{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "תאכלו", :translit "to'kheilu", :gloss "you may eat"} {:he "וכל אשר אין לו", :translit "ve-khol asher ein lo", :gloss "and all that does not have to it"}], :mechanements [], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "juxtaposition (semikhut): the word 'you may eat' placed immediately before the phrase 'whatever lacks' to derive a permissive ruling"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'whatever lacks' (וכל אשר אין לו) in Deuteronomy 14:9 is read as continuing from the preceding command 'you may eat' (תאכלו). Since an unborn fish fetus found inside a ritually impure mother fish is itself a distinct creature, the juxtaposition is read as a license to eat such a fetus if it bears the kosher signs of a pure fish. This reflects the halakhic principle that a fetus of a non-kosher species may be permitted if found alive and possessing its own kosher characteristics.", :sources ["Chullin 67b" "Deuteronomy 14:9"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "juxtaposition (semikhut): the word 'you may eat' placed immediately before the phrase 'whatever lacks' to derive a permissive ruling", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:34.994243206Z", :model "glm-5.2"}